-- title:
--   Level automorphisms preserve the j-finite chart and fix y
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q\ge 5$, a nonzero $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell\ge 3$ with $\ell\ne q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a $(q\ell)$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, with $\zeta\in L$ a primitive $q$-th and $\xi\in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L\subseteq \operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ obtained as `laurentBaseChange`, i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field `xHFunctionField` of level $N_0=(q\ell)^2M'$ for the subgroup $H=\ker\big((\mathbb{Z}/N_0)^\times\to(\mathbb{Z}/q\ell)^\times\big)$ of units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $q$ in its maximal ideal, $\zeta$ in the image of $A$, and with a compatible $A$-algebra structure on $K$; let $\varpi$ generate the maximal ideal. Let $j\in K$, $j\ne 0$, have Laurent series the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant. Write $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}=$ `chartAlgFin` for the subalgebra of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, and let $X$ be the two-chart integral model, the pushout of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the two inclusions of $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ and of the integral closure of $A[j^{-1}]$ into the middle chart. Let $z\in X$ be such that the germ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under the global sections of the structure morphism $X\to\operatorname{Spec} A$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk at $z$, and let $y\in\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ map to $z$ under the canonical morphism, with the supersingularity hypothesis: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}\to\Omega$ with kernel the prime $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in `ssJSet` $q\,\Omega$, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$. Then two assertions hold. First, for every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $\tau\in\operatorname{Aut}_L(K)$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` for $L$, $q\ell$, $\xi$, $q\ell$, $N_0$, $H$ and $\gamma^{-1}$ — that is, $\tau$ carries each quotient of two modular forms of level $\Gamma_H(N_0)$ with integral $q$-expansions to the corresponding quotient of their translates by `conjElemN` $(q\ell)\,\gamma^{-1}$, as read off under any embedding $L\hookrightarrow\mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$ — one has $\tau(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}})\subseteq\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}$. Second, if in addition $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, then for the restriction of $\tau$ to $\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ and every $a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{fin}}$ one has $\tau(a)-a\in y$.
--
--   This packages the two clauses needed for the study of the level-$(q\ell)^2M'$ integral model at a supersingular point in characteristic $q$: level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserve the chart algebra of elements integral over $A[j]$, and those attached to $\Gamma(\ell)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ act trivially on the residue data at the prime $y$, the latter being stronger than mere fixing of the closed point. It is used by the statements producing ring isomorphisms of the completed stalk at the Drinfeld chart and describing the inertia action there.
